    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>German merchants founded Berlin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a> on either side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, and the two towns merged in 1432; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> made the city their residence in the 15th century and ruled it until 1918.</li><li>Berlin became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1701 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871, and by the 1920s it was the third-largest city in the world and a major centre of science, art and industry.</li><li>After the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city was divided into four sectors and then, from 1961 to 1989, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a>, and it became the capital of a reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1990.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Berlin is of Slavic origin and probably means something like &quot;swamp&quot;; despite the bear on the city's coat of arms, it is not related to the German word for bear.</p></aside>
    </section>

