    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1237</span><span class="rf-text">The year later taken as the founding date of Berlin, from the first recorded mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1432</span><span class="rf-text">Berlin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a> merge into the single town of Berlin-Cölln</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1701</span><span class="rf-text">Frederick I makes Berlin the capital of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1871</span><span class="rf-text">Berlin becomes the capital of the newly founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text">The Greater Berlin Act incorporates surrounding towns and villages and roughly doubles the population</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The Battle of Berlin ends with the city's capitulation and its division into four Allied sectors</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1961</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> begins to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Berlin are reunited, and Berlin again becomes the capital of the whole country</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear.jpg" alt="Albert the Bear" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adalbertus_Siegel.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Berlin lies in low-lying marshy woodland on the Northern European Plain, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, which flows through a glacial valley and empties into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spandau">Spandau</a>. The area has been settled for millennia: a deer mask dated to 9,000 BCE is attributed to the Maglemosian culture, and around 2,000 BCE dense settlement along the Spree and Havel gave rise to the Lusatian culture. From about 500 BCE Germanic tribes settled in villages on the higher ground, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semnones">Semnones</a> left around 200 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> followed. In the 7th century Slavic tribes, later known as the Hevelli and Sprevane, reached the region, with main settlements at Spandau and Köpenick.</p><p>In the 12th century the region came under German rule as part of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> in 1157. At the end of that century German merchants founded two settlements in what is now the city centre: Berlin, also called Alt-Berlin, on the site of the present Nikolaiviertel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a>, on the island in the Spree now known as Museum Island. It is unclear which settlement is older or when each received German town rights. The year 1237 was later taken as the founding date because Cölln was first mentioned then; Berlin was first mentioned in 1244 and as a town in 1251, and Cölln as a town in 1261. The name Berlin is of Slavic origin and is usually interpreted as "swamp".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Luycx_-_Frederick_William,_Elector_of_Brandenburg,_at_three-quarter-length.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Frans Luycx, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The two towns profited from the staple right on two important trade routes, one known as the Via Imperii and the other running from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> to Novgorod. They formed an alliance in 1307 with a common external policy, though their internal administrations stayed separate, and in 1432 they merged formally into the town of Berlin-Cölln. By 1400 they had 8,000 inhabitants. A great fire in 1380 damaged most of the written records of those early years.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a> made Berlin their residence in the 15th century the city lost its status as a free Hanseatic city, and its main activity shifted from trade to the production of luxury goods for the court. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">the Great Elector</a>, Frederick William, who ruled from 1640, new suburbs such as Friedrichswerder and Dorotheenstadt were founded, and Berlin reached 20,000 inhabitants and became significant among the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> for the first time. After 1701 the city grew alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Under Frederick William I it took on the character of a garrison town and armoury, and in 1720 he founded the Charité, the city's first major hospital and medical school. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> then transformed it: Berlin pioneered the use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a> after 1815, became the main railway hub of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and made textiles, machinery, railway equipment and, after 1880, electrical machinery. The population rose from 172,000 in 1800 to 826,000 in 1870 and to 1,888,000 in 1900.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II, Elector of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arolsen_Klebeband_01_203_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>'s Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> ruled the region from the 12th century. In 1415 Frederick I became elector of Brandenburg, and members of the Hohenzollern family ruled in Berlin until 1918, first as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>, then as kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and finally as German emperors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II</a> began a new royal palace in 1443, and the citizens' protest against it, the "Berlin Indignation" of 1448, failed and cost them many of their political and economic privileges. The palace became the permanent residence of the Brandenburg electors, and the electors and the city became Lutheran in 1539.</p><p>In 1701 Elector Frederick III crowned himself Frederick I in Prussia and made Berlin the capital of the new Kingdom of Prussia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> reigned from 1740, and the city was briefly occupied by the Russian army during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. French troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> entered Berlin in 1806 but granted it self-government. Frederick William IV suppressed the revolution of 1848 and limited the vote to about 5 percent of the population, a system that lasted until 1918.</p><p>After the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1870 and 1871 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> was founded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> as emperor and Bismarck as chancellor, and Berlin became its capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a> replaced the empire in 1918, and Berlin remained the capital until 1945, under Nazi rule from 1933. East Berlin was declared the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> in 1949, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>'s government. Berlin again became the capital of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1990.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great.jpg" alt="Frederick the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_der_Gro%C3%9Fe_-_Johann_Georg_Ziesenis_-_Google_Cultural_Institute_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johann Georg Ziesenis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Immigration shaped the city early. In 1671 fifty Jewish families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> were given a home in Berlin, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam">Edict of Potsdam</a> of 1685 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">the Great Elector</a> invited the French Calvinist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. More than 15,000 came, of whom 6,000 settled in Berlin, and by 1687 they made up 20 percent of the population; many were bankers, industrialists and investors. Refugees also arrived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>. Frederick William I encouraged Protestants from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> to settle and introduced universal primary education. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> Berlin became a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and Moses Mendelssohn was among the thinkers he patronised. The university, now the Humboldt University, was founded in 1810 with the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte">Johann Gottlieb Fichte</a> as its first rector, and in 1812 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were granted the right to practise all occupations.</p><p>By 1900 about 85 percent of Berliners were classified as Protestants, 10 percent as Roman Catholics and 5 percent as Jews. Workers moved into tenement blocks in districts such as Kreuzberg, Prenzlauer Berg, Friedrichshain and Wedding, and the city's labour movement supported radical socialism. The imperial city built a modern sewer system in the late 1870s and began its underground railway, the U-Bahn, in 1896. In the 1920s Berlin was a leading cultural centre: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-gropius">Walter Gropius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a>, George Grosz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertolt-brecht">Bertolt Brecht</a> and others worked there, and its nightlife flourished.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg.jpg" alt="Rosa Luxemburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Luxemburg_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Berlin suffered repeatedly. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> of 1618 to 1648 damaged a third of its houses and cost it half its population, and bubonic plague had killed about 6,000 people in 1576. In 1510, 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were accused of stealing and desecrating hosts; 38 were burned to death and the rest were banished and lost their possessions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought food shortages, and the "turnip winter" of 1917 to 1918 was remembered for generations. In the revolution that began on 9 November 1918, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartacist-uprising">Spartacist uprising</a> of January 1919 failed and the Communist leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> and Karl Liebknecht were killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">Freikorps</a> members; the March battles of 1919 cost about 1,200 lives. The Kapp–Lüttwitz Putsch of March 1920 failed within five days, and Walther Rathenau was assassinated in 1922. The Great Depression raised unemployment in Berlin to 630,000 by December 1932.</p><p>After Hitler came to power in 1933 the Jewish community, some 160,000 people around that year, was persecuted and destroyed; after Kristallnacht in 1938 thousands were imprisoned, and from early 1943 many were deported to camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>. Allied bombing from 1943 and the Battle of Berlin in 1945 followed. Hitler killed himself on 30 April 1945, and Berlin capitulated on 2 May. Up to a third of the city had been destroyed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had dropped 67,607 tons of bombs on it according to one source and 75,000 tons according to the other.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin-wall.jpg" alt="Berlin Wall" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berlinermauer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Noir, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After 1945 Berlin was divided into American, British, French and Soviet sectors. When the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> extended a currency reform to their sectors, the Soviets blocked ground access to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a> on 26 June 1948, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift">Berlin Airlift</a> supplied the city until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> ended on 11 May 1949. On 13 August 1961 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> began to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> around West Berlin. During its existence around 5,000 people escaped into West Berlin, and 136 were officially killed trying to cross. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> gave his "Ich bin ein Berliner" speech on 26 June 1963, and a Four-Power Agreement of 1971 guaranteed access to West Berlin.</p><p>The Wall fell on 9 November 1989, after a press conference announced that border restrictions would be lifted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Berlin were reunited on 3 October 1990. In 1991 the Bundestag voted narrowly, by 338 to 320, to move the German capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> to Berlin, and the move was largely completed in 1999. The Palast der Republik was torn down to make way for the Humboldt Forum, a reconstruction of the Berlin Palace, and Potsdamer Platz was rebuilt.</p><p>Berlin is today the capital and largest city of Germany, with 3.7 million inhabitants, and is one of the country's states. Its Museum Island is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and it has the largest Turkish community outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Berlin grew from the twin merchant towns of Berlin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colln">Cölln</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a>, founded in the 13th century. It became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1701 and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871, was divided from 1945 to 1990, and is now the capital of reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</em></p>

